\section{Known limits}{

\verb{\\Sexpr[results=rd]} produces Rd that is itself parsed and may carry further
code; that second-order surface is not followed.

\code{tools::parse_Rd()} recovers from some malformed input with a warning and a
truncated tree. Whatever was recovered is still returned, since dropping it
would lose real code, and the warning is reported in \code{error}.

The \code{examples} string is not guaranteed to parse. R never syntax-checks
\verb{\dontrun{}}, so including that code exposes blocks that are not valid R.
The string is assembled whole, so one broken \verb{\dontrun{}} costs the valid
example code in the same file.
